3. Start Jenkins and Tomcat
Step 3.1 – Open Jenkins
Open your browser:

http://localhost:8080/jenkins

Or, if Jenkins runs independently:

http://localhost:8080

Log in using your Jenkins username and password.

Note: If Tomcat already uses port 8080, Jenkins must use a different port, such as 8081.

Step 3.2 – Start Tomcat
If you're using Windows:

Open your Tomcat installation folder.

Open the bin folder.

Double-click startup.bat.

Or run:

cd C:\apache-tomcat-9\bin
startup.bat
Open:

http://localhost:8080

You should see the Tomcat homepage.

If Jenkins also uses port 8080, change one of the ports before starting both services.

4. Configure Tomcat for Jenkins deployment
Jenkins needs permission to deploy applications to Tomcat.

Open:

C:\apache-tomcat-9\conf\tomcat-users.xml
Inside the tomcat-users element, add:

<role rolename="manager-script"/>
<user username="admin"
      password="admin123"
      roles="manager-script"/>

Save the file.

Restart Tomcat:

shutdown.bat
startup.bat
These credentials are just an example for a local lab. Use strong credentials in a real deployment.

5. Install Jenkins plugins
Open Jenkins.

Go to:

Dashboard → Manage Jenkins → Plugins → Available plugins

Search for and install:

Git plugin

Maven Integration plugin (optional for the method below)

Deploy to container plugin

Install them and restart Jenkins if requested.

The Deploy to container plugin allows Jenkins to deploy WAR files to supported application servers, including Tomcat. 

Jenkins plugin
