9. Configure the build
Scroll down to the Build Steps section.

Click:

Add build step → Execute Windows batch command

If Jenkins runs on Windows, enter:

mvn clean package
If Jenkins runs on Linux, choose Execute shell and enter:

mvn clean package

This command:

Downloads required Maven dependencies.

Compiles your Java source code.

Packages your project.

Generates the WAR file.

Expected output
After a successful Maven build, you should find something like:

workspace/
   your-project/
      target/
         your-project.war
Important: This assumes your Git repository contains a Maven pom.xml configured to package a WAR. If it generates a JAR instead, or doesn't contain a Java web application, we'll need a different build/deployment configuration.

10. Configure WAR deployment to Tomcat
This is the most important step!

Scroll down to Post-build Actions.

Click Add post-build action.

Select Deploy war/ear to a container.

You should see deployment configuration fields.

Enter the following:

Field

Value

WAR/EAR files

**/target/*.war

Context path

myapp

Container

Tomcat 9.x

Credentials

tomcat-credentials

Tomcat URL

http://localhost:8080


Important: The Tomcat version in the deployment configuration must match the server you're running. The plugin documents Tomcat 9 support; for Tomcat 10, use a compatible deployment method and ensure your application supports Jakarta Servlet APIs.

Click Apply → Save.

